  "facts": [
    "The George Washington Bridge, opened in 1931, held the world record for the longest suspension span—3,500 feet—until the Golden Gate Bridge eclipsed it in 1937, proving longer spans were economically viable.",
    "Designed by Othmar Ammann, the bridge carries 14 vehicular lanes, more than any other suspension bridge, with an upper deck widened from six to eight lanes in 1946 to accommodate growing traffic.",
    "Construction required 28,000 short tons of wire, stretching 106,000 miles, and 113,000 short tons of fabricated steel, illustrating the massive engineering effort behind this iconic Hudson crossing."
  ],
